  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe gloomy weather pattern will continue as cyclonic flow aloft\npersists over the northeastern CONUS through much of the\nweekend. The current round of lake effect rain showers will\nlargely taper off through early this morning before shower\ncoverage increases as the next piece of energy pivots across the\nregion later this morning into this afternoon. By Friday night,\nsteering flow will shift to the northwest and focus the\nprecipitation into Northeast Ohio. There will be plenty of\nlake-induced instability with lake temperatures right around 13C\nand 850mb temps likely falling to around -6C tonight, so precip\nrates may be efficient at times. Expect rain as the primary \nptype through early this evening before mixing with and possibly\neven transitioning to snow after 7 PM/00Z tonight. The main\nchallenge with the forecast is how cold temperatures get\novernight tonight; northwest flow off of the (relatively) warm\nlake will most likely keep temperatures just above freezing.\nWith that in mind, snow will likely struggle to accumulate. The\ncurrent forecast has a trace to roughly half an inch across the\nCentral Highlands and into the secondary snowbelt areas of \ninterior NE OH/NW PA with locally higher amounts of around an \ninch possible. A few high res models suggest that higher \namounts of at least a couple inches are possible where bands of \nsnow persist, especially in the event of a Lake Huron moisture \nconnection, but there is a great deal of uncertainty in where \nexactly that could occur since it will probably be very \nisolated. Will need to keep an eye on guidance trends, \nespecially forecast overnight temperatures trend even a couple \nof degrees cooler. Either way, impacts to travel aren't expected\nat this point since wet snow accumulations will largely be \nconfined to grassy and elevated surfaces.\n\nPrecip will transition back to rain during the day Saturday as\ntemperatures warm into the upper 30s and lower 40s and rain\nchances will likely increase across NW PA during this time. A\nfew wet snowflakes may mix with rain in NW PA late Saturday \nnight, but a cold rain is generally favored as the upper trough \nslowly starts to push east of the area and 850mb temps \nincrease. Expect precipitation to gradually dwindle throughout \nthe day Thursday as 850mb moisture decreases and a ridge starts \nbuilding in from the west.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will build into the region by early next week with\ndry weather expected Monday onwards. Temperatures will slowly\nincrease throughout the week as the high gradually shifts east\nwith highs in the mid to upper 40s on Monday giving way to above\nnormal mid to upper 50s by Thursday.",
